parts.text:
bindVariable works with (nodeId, field, varId). Good.

Now do the bulk update + bind. Need to find page roots and off-scale nodes. Let's inspect nodes with the target values and names:
- itemSpacing 18: Typography.
- itemSpacing 64: Showcase.
- padding 48: Foundations, Component library, Showcase.

Use eval to update them:
```
for node if name==='Foundations'||'Component library'||'Showcase' && type FRAME && paddingTop===48: updateNode(id,{paddingTop:44,...})
if name==='Typography' && itemSpacing===18: updateNode(...16)
if name==='Showcase' && itemSpacing===64: updateNode(...52)
```
Then bind.

Bulk bind algorithm details:
```
const spaceVars = {...}
const ctrlPadH = varByName['control.padding.horizontal'], ctrlPadV=..., toggleGap, sliderGap, stepperGap
const controlSets = /^(Button|TextField|Select|Date|Color|Menu)( Dark)?$/
function setAncestor(n){...}
function hasInstanceAncestor(n){...}
for (const n of nodes) {
  if(hasInstanceAncestor(n)) continue;
  const set=setAncestor(n)?.name ?? '';
  const isControl = controlSets.test(set);
  for field of ['itemSpacing','counterAxisSpacing','gridColumnGap','gridRowGap','paddingTop','paddingRight','paddingBottom','paddingLeft']:
    v=n[field]; if !num or 0 skip; if n.boundVariables?.[field] skip;
    let vid=null;
    if(field has 'padding' && isControl){ if(v===12) vid=ctrlPadH.id; else if(v===6) vid=ctrlPadV.id; }
    if(!vid && field==='itemSpacing'){
      if(/^Slider( Dark)?$/.test(set) && v===10) vid=sliderGap.id;
      else if(/^Stepper( Dark)?$/.test(set) && v===8) vid=stepperGap.id;
      else if(n.name && n.name.startsWith('Toggle ') && v===8) vid=toggleGap.id;
    }
    if(!vid) vid = spaceVars[v];
    if(vid) g.bindVariable(n.id, field, vid);
}
```
But `control.padding.horizontal` is 12 and `control.padding.vertical` 6. Wait `control.padding.vertical` = 6? Yes. However, in the input/button components we used px=12, py=6? For Button we used px only, height 36. For TextField Input h36 px12. Select px12. Menu trigger px12. So horizontal 12, vertical padding 0 (height). For Item px12, h36. So only horizontal. The vertical 6 appears in icons? Button py not used; earlier probe used py but final generator button uses only px and h36. Some nodes p=6 (color swatch p6) inside Color component (set name 'Color'), so padding all sides 6 → control.padding.vertical (6) and control.padding.horizontal is 12 not 6! If we bind all padding 6 in a control to ctrlPadV (6), okay; for horizontal padding 6 it would bind to vertical 6 (same value). Semantically odd. But Color component p6 means all sides 6; control.padding.vertical=6 is the only 6 token. Could instead bind padding 6 generic to base.space.1_5. Hmm.

Let's simplify control padding mapping:
- For paddingLeft/Right with v===12 → control.padding.horizontal.
- For paddingTop/Bottom with v===6 → control.padding.vertical.
- Otherwise fallback base space.

Color swatch p6: left/right 6 → fallback base.space.1_5 (6); top/bottom 6 → control.padding.vertical (6) if isControl. That's inconsistent (sides base, top/bottom control). Better: for Color component, p6 all sides should bind to base.space.1_5 uniformly (since it's a swatch inset, not control padding). We could exclude Color/Menu from control mapping? Menu trigger uses px12 (control padding horizontal) and popover item px12. Keep Menu.
- Control padding mapping applies to Button, TextField, Select, Date, Menu (horizontal 12 only). Color excluded.
- For horizontal 12 and vertical 6, use control tokens only in those sets.
- For all other, base.space.

Also the dark variants of Button etc. get control.padding.* too, mirroring light.

What about the button in Stepper ('Button' child frames, 28x28) with no padding; no issue.

What about `Menu` popover p4 (padding all sides 4): set name Menu, horizontal 4 not 12 → fallback base.space.1 (4). Top/bottom 4 → fallback base.space.1. Good.

Toggle gap mapping: showcase nodes named "Toggle Switch on" etc. have itemSpacing 8. Also the component sets "Switch theme pair" has itemSpacing 24; base.space.6. The "Switch" component set itemSpacing 16 (from setEl) → base.space.4. Good.
Stepper set: itemSpacing 16 (setEl) and component itemSpacing 8 → stepper.gap for component (set ancestor name 'Stepper'/'Stepper Dark'), and set gap 16 → base.
Slider set: itemSpacing 16 and component 10 → slider.gap.
Note setAncestor for the component variant node itself: parent is COMPONENT_SET, so set name matches. Good. For children inside the component (Track, Thumb), set ancestor is also the set; their itemSpacing maybe 0 or none. Fine.

But `hasInstanceAncestor` should skip all nodes inside instance subtrees, including the instance node itself? The instance node type INSTANCE; we can skip if any ancestor (including self) is INSTANCE. For instance node, no layout props typically. Fine.

Panel names: "Light panel"/"Dark panel" have p20/gap12; bind base.space.5/3.
Section frames itemSpacing 16 → base.space.4. Root gap 40 → base.space.10.
Showcase sections gap16 → base.4.
Buttons row gap12 → base.3.
Fields col gap8 → base.2.
etc.

What about `paddingRight/Left:12` already bound to control.padding.horizontal (20 nodes) — skip. Other 12s bind base.space.3.
What about `itemSpacing:2` 41 nodes (Meta gap2) → base.space.0_5.
All good.

After binding, count bound/unbound and report. Then verify no visual changes by comparing export? Since values unchanged (except 48→44 etc.), layout shifts slightly. Let's export showcase and components maybe. But binding shouldn't change values. Off-scale changes affect page padding and board gap; fine.

Also need to fix `textCase` after? No new text nodes. The document saved has UPPER. Good. But the saved file has some textCase UPPER; our eval won't alter.

Potential issue: save validation might now complain about variable binding on fields not allowed? Binding itemSpacing to FLOAT variable is valid. Save should work.

Let's implement. Also update the generator file to reflect off-scale changes and maybe add spacing binding logic if regenerated in future. But the deliverable is the fig; still, keeping generator in temp is irrelevant. Since the generator is only in temp, no repo changes. We can skip updating it. But for reproducibility, not needed.

Wait, the user might later run the generator? It's not in the repo. Fine.

Let's run the update + bind.

